WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.870
Narrator: In 1923's
"The Ten Commandments,"

00:00:02.870 --> 00:00:05.660
the Israelites walk
through the parted Red Sea.

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:09.310
It's actually two separate
shots combined into one.

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:11.220
According to sources at the time,

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:13.550
the first shot was just the actors,

00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:16.780
and the second was actually gelatin.

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:19.230
This compositing technique was a precursor

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:21.340
to the blue- and green-screen technology

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:23.440
that has taken over the industry.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.082
Julie Turnock: There's
almost nothing that is

00:00:25.082 --> 00:00:29.060
completely unrelated to a previous event.

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:31.840
Narrator: Back before the
widespread use of CGI,

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.360
effects artists and
filmmakers did everything

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:36.320
from painting on top of shots

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:38.090
to projecting moving backgrounds

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:40.370
to make the impossible happen.

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:42.660
Here are seven tricks
used in old Hollywood

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:45.435
that still exist in some form today.

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:50.360
CG allows filmmakers to
place people and objects

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:52.030
anywhere they want in a scene,

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:56.220
but it wasn't used in a movie
until 1973's "Westworld."

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:00.160
So how did filmmakers in
the early 1900s do this?

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.110
With arguably the
first-ever special effect,

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:05.350
the substitution splice.

00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:07.940
A filmmaker would start filming a scene.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:09.265
Then they'd stop the camera

00:01:09.265 --> 00:01:11.348
and start shooting again
from the same position

00:01:11.348 --> 00:01:14.140
with a new person or
object added to the frame.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:15.940
This effect made it seem like something

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:18.510
or someone appeared out of thin air.

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:21.120
Director Georges Méliès
used a substitution splice

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.020
in many of his on-screen illusions,

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:26.790
such as this one in 1896's
"The Vanishing Lady."

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:29.900
The trick was also used to
pull off dangerous stunts.

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:31.447
Take a look at 1895's

00:01:31.447 --> 00:01:33.890
"The Execution of Mary, Queen of Scots."

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:35.730
To make the monarch's beheading look real,

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:37.930
the actor first knelt
at the chopping block,

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:39.530
then the director stopped filming

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:42.190
and a dummy was placed
where the actor was.

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:43.280
When they resumed filming,

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:46.060
the executioner chopped
off the dummy's head.

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:48.510
By today's standards,
this early form of editing

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:50.240
is pretty noticeable.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:52.860
Stunt coordinators these
days improve this concept

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:54.450
with camera lock-offs.

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:56.178
Take this moment, when
The Rock's character

00:01:56.178 --> 00:01:58.080
gets hit in the face by a motorcycle

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.880
in 2016's "Central Intelligence."

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:02.010
To keep the actor safe,

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:04.700
they filmed the shot from
the exact same angle twice.

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:07.640
First they captured a
motorcyclist doing a backflip,

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:10.270
then The Rock's stunt
double being pulled back.

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:12.190
With today's visual effects technology,

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:14.838
the two takes could be
seamlessly combined.

00:02:16.088 --> 00:02:18.210
One of the earliest
challenges of animation

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:19.790
was natural movement.

00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:22.030
So animators turned to rotoscoping,

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:24.640
first thought up by
Max and Dave Fleischer.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:26.880
Artists would project
live-action film footage

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:30.025
onto their easel and sketch
over the images frame by frame

00:02:30.025 --> 00:02:32.230
so they could mimic real-life movements.

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:35.550
For example, in the 1932
cartoon "Minnie the Moocher,"

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:37.422
the Fleischer brothers gave the characters

00:02:37.422 --> 00:02:38.820
eye-popping dance moves

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:41.220
by rotoscoping Cab Calloway.

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:44.120
Nowadays, rotoscoping software
allows for this process

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:47.220
to be used on something much
longer than a short cartoon,

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:49.330
like the TV series "Undone."

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:51.750
And motion-capture tech
is a natural extension

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:53.790
of the Fleischers' work,
with fully realized

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:56.805
mo-cap characters like
Thanos in "Infinity War."

00:02:58.371 --> 00:03:01.020
1933's "The Invisible Man" needed to shoot

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:03.100
its invisible lead wearing clothes

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:04.780
so the audience could see him.

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:06.380
How do you create a floating shirt

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:08.450
without today's technology?

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:09.700
Instead of a green screen,

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:12.150
they used a technique called compositing.

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:13.692
In Méliès' time, this could be done

00:03:13.692 --> 00:03:15.210
through double exposure.

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:17.520
But James Whale instead
used a compositing method

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:19.650
known as the Williams process.

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:22.540
First, actor Claude Rains
was filmed in black velvet

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:24.180
against a black backdrop.

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:25.788
This created a negative,

00:03:25.788 --> 00:03:27.938
allowing every part of
the actor to disappear

00:03:27.938 --> 00:03:30.530
except for the parts not covered in black.

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:32.780
They then filmed the
background separately.

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:34.350
These mattes were then printed,

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:36.420
masking off the foreground and background

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:38.980
until the two images were combined.

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:40.615
Creating the right composite images

00:03:40.615 --> 00:03:43.220
often involved thinking outside the box,

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:44.910
like that scene from
"The Ten Commandments"

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:46.650
we described earlier.

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:48.710
Because the compositing was done by hand,

00:03:48.710 --> 00:03:50.510
it was an imperfect process.

00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:53.570
If you watch old movies in
which you can see the edges

00:03:53.570 --> 00:03:56.890
around the figures where
they've been composited in,

00:03:56.890 --> 00:03:59.260
that means that the
optical printer operator

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:01.010
was short on time.

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:02.210
Oftentimes it's just cheap effects,

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:04.720
because they didn't want
to spend money to do it.

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:07.240
Narrator: Modern compositing
is much smoother.

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:10.400
The black screen used in
1933's "Invisible Man"

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:13.610
has been replaced with today's
blue and green screens,

00:04:13.610 --> 00:04:16.180
and elements are filmed
on motion-control cameras

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:17.890
for a more seamless composite,

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:20.030
like in 2020's "The Invisible Man."

00:04:20.030 --> 00:04:21.979
They first shot one take of Elisabeth Moss

00:04:21.979 --> 00:04:23.620
being attacked by a stand-in

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:26.060
and then a second clean take without them.

00:04:27.988 --> 00:04:29.730
Things get a bit more complicated

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:31.700
when it comes to fantasy backgrounds,

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:33.140
like in "The Wizard of Oz."

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:36.010
The filmmakers couldn't just
composite in real footage

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:37.190
for the land of Oz,

00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:39.080
and building all the sets from scratch

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:41.350
would have been a difficult task.

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:43.260
So instead they used matte paintings

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:45.750
to paint around the characters.

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:47.700
For this effect, a matte
blocking everything

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:50.510
except the actors was placed
in front of the camera.

00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:52.570
The artists later painted the surroundings

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:54.153
on a separate glass matte

00:04:54.153 --> 00:04:56.486
before the two images were combined.

00:04:56.486 --> 00:04:58.604
So, for example, when Dorothy walks down

00:04:58.604 --> 00:05:00.080
the Yellow Brick Road in this shot,

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:03.800
it was actually just the actors
on a set with no backdrop.

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:05.558
The sky, flowers, and mountains

00:05:05.558 --> 00:05:07.383
were all painted in after the fact

00:05:07.383 --> 00:05:08.550
to make it look like they were headed

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:10.230
towards the Emerald City.

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:13.400
But it's still hard to overcome
the flatness of a painting.

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:15.380
Look at this shot of Orson Welles speaking

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:18.060
in front of a large
crowd in "Citizen Kane."

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:21.513
The painted extras appear
completely lifeless.

00:05:21.513 --> 00:05:23.880
Present-day digital
mattes and set extensions

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:25.260
offer a fix to that.

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:27.220
By duplicating tiles of real extras

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:29.190
and adding digital doubles into the mix,

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:31.770
"Ted Lasso" could create
a fully formed stadium

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:34.488
filled with 26,000 fans who could react

00:05:34.488 --> 00:05:37.030
directly to the action shot in-camera.

00:05:38.990 --> 00:05:40.900
Without today's animation software,

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:42.928
it can be difficult to add realistic

00:05:42.928 --> 00:05:45.730
three-dimensional depth
to hand-drawn scenes.

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:47.580
So in the 1930s and '40s,

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:50.030
many animated Disney
classics used an invention

00:05:50.030 --> 00:05:52.410
called a multiplane camera.

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:54.900
The camera was attached to
several layers of glass,

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:57.130
each displaying a
different layer of artwork.

00:05:57.130 --> 00:05:59.192
Each layer could be
moved at different speeds

00:05:59.192 --> 00:06:00.970
and distances from each other.

00:06:00.970 --> 00:06:03.120
Because the two-dimensional
drawings were separated

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:05.020
in real three-dimensional space,

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:07.280
they created a parallax effect.

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:09.890
This camera helped add depth
to the forest in "Bambi,"

00:06:09.890 --> 00:06:11.240
and it's how filmmakers made

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:13.465
the Evil Queen's
surroundings spin around her

00:06:13.465 --> 00:06:15.540
in "Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs."

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:17.430
These days, the multiplane-camera effect

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:19.110
is recreated digitally.

00:06:19.110 --> 00:06:20.710
The various layers can be designed

00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:22.468
and easily separated on a computer

00:06:22.468 --> 00:06:24.340
to create the illusion of depth,

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:26.150
and filmmakers are getting more creative

00:06:26.150 --> 00:06:29.350
with how they integrate 3D
assets and trick the eye.

00:06:29.350 --> 00:06:32.980
2020's "Wolfwalkers" created
a 3D forest as a guide

00:06:32.980 --> 00:06:36.963
to place hand-drawn characters
into dynamic environments.

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:41.215
Even before CG, filmmakers tried to fix

00:06:41.215 --> 00:06:44.390
the static nature of painted
mattes and 2D backgrounds.

00:06:44.390 --> 00:06:46.440
One solution was rear projection,

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:49.890
which created dynamic moving
backgrounds in real time.

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:51.580
An actor would stand in front of a screen,

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:52.760
and behind that screen,

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:55.500
a projector would play
footage shot elsewhere.

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:58.630
Like this shot from
1938's "Bringing up Baby."

00:06:58.630 --> 00:07:00.850
In reality, the two stars and this leopard

00:07:00.850 --> 00:07:02.620
were nowhere near each other.

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:05.150
Cary Grant and Katharine
Hepburn were actually driving

00:07:05.150 --> 00:07:06.940
in a fake car on a set,

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:08.610
while the leopard was shot separately

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:11.230
and projected behind them on a screen.

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:14.630
Or this plane crash from
1940's "Foreign Correspondent."

00:07:14.630 --> 00:07:17.050
Director Alfred Hitchcock
projected real footage

00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:20.160
of the sky and ocean through
the cockpit's windows.

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:22.383
But rear projection had its own issues.

00:07:22.383 --> 00:07:24.137
Because it was projected from the back,

00:07:24.137 --> 00:07:26.970
the lighting and image
looked a lot less realistic.

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:28.650
Plus, it was difficult
to match the lighting

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:30.950
of the real world in the studio.

00:07:30.950 --> 00:07:33.110
That's why the background
roads in these driving scenes

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:37.352
from "To Catch a Thief" and
"Dr. No" look so flat today.

00:07:37.352 --> 00:07:39.580
Rear projection also
became harder to utilize

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:41.912
as more and more films were shot in color.

00:07:41.912 --> 00:07:44.750
The colors are often just a little off.

00:07:44.750 --> 00:07:47.680
And they're just off enough
that, especially today,

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:49.670
because, you know, with
digital composites,

00:07:49.670 --> 00:07:51.610
you can correct that stuff pretty easily,

00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:53.200
but you couldn't at the time.

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:54.940
Narrator: Still, rear projection lives on

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:56.650
through boundary-pushing technologies

00:07:56.650 --> 00:08:00.450
used by directors like Stanley
Kubrick and Jon Favreau.

00:08:00.450 --> 00:08:02.100
In "2001: A Space Odyssey,"

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:03.860
Kubrick instead placed the projector

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:05.126
in front of the screen

00:08:05.126 --> 00:08:07.151
with a light bouncing off a mirror.

00:08:07.151 --> 00:08:09.850
This created a stunning
deep focus and perspective

00:08:09.850 --> 00:08:11.770
during the Dawn of Man sequence.

00:08:11.770 --> 00:08:13.450
You won't see that in a rear projection

00:08:13.450 --> 00:08:15.070
or any matte painting.

00:08:15.070 --> 00:08:17.210
And LED screens have turned projection

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:20.210
from a cheesy effect to awe-inspiring.

00:08:20.210 --> 00:08:23.140
For example, "The Mandalorian"
chose to swap projections

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:25.715
and green screens with LED screens.

00:08:25.715 --> 00:08:28.130
You can immediately see how
naturally the light reflects

00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:30.850
off the Mandalorian and
Baby Yoda in this shot.

00:08:30.850 --> 00:08:32.585
And because the camera actually moves

00:08:32.585 --> 00:08:34.095
in sync with the background,

00:08:34.095 --> 00:08:36.460
this shot has a natural parallax effect,

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:39.869
just like those multiplane
cameras from decades earlier.

00:08:41.830 --> 00:08:43.470
Before monsters and other creatures

00:08:43.470 --> 00:08:46.020
could be created with
CG and motion capture,

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:49.370
filmmakers primarily used
stop-motion animation,

00:08:49.370 --> 00:08:50.520
but stop motion requires

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:53.003
expensive miniature models and sets,

00:08:53.003 --> 00:08:54.190
which often look unnatural

00:08:54.190 --> 00:08:55.970
when interacting with the cast.

00:08:55.970 --> 00:08:57.860
You can see that from
looking at "King Kong,"

00:08:57.860 --> 00:08:59.101
as the claymation Kong

00:08:59.101 --> 00:09:01.284
can't truly interact with Fay Wray.

00:09:01.284 --> 00:09:04.520
So pioneering stop-motion
animator Ray Harryhausen

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:06.610
thought of Dynamation.

00:09:06.610 --> 00:09:09.990
His method combined a range
of visual effects techniques,

00:09:09.990 --> 00:09:11.810
from mattes to projection.

00:09:11.810 --> 00:09:14.100
To understand how it works,
let's look at this fight scene

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:16.570
between human characters
and a skeleton army

00:09:16.570 --> 00:09:19.505
in 1963's "Jason and the Argonauts."

00:09:19.505 --> 00:09:21.770
The actors rehearsed their
moves with stuntpeople

00:09:21.770 --> 00:09:23.920
who stood in for the skeleton army,

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:25.420
but the scene was actually shot

00:09:25.420 --> 00:09:27.203
without the stunt performers

00:09:27.203 --> 00:09:29.910
and only with the actors
playing the human characters.

00:09:29.910 --> 00:09:33.320
Harryhausen then used stop
motion for the skeleton army.

00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:34.750
To match the movements perfectly,

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:36.621
the footage of the actors shadowboxing

00:09:36.621 --> 00:09:38.838
was projected behind the animation table

00:09:38.838 --> 00:09:42.080
while Harryhausen moved the
skeletons frame by frame.

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:44.822
A camera filmed the skeleton
army through a glass matte,

00:09:44.822 --> 00:09:47.130
masking off the shadowboxing actors.

00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:50.080
Finally, the shadowboxing
scene and the skeleton scenes

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:52.380
were printed and composited together.

00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:53.813
This five-minute sequence

00:09:53.813 --> 00:09:56.496
took four and a half months to complete.

00:09:56.496 --> 00:09:58.610
But Dynamation's influence
can still be seen

00:09:58.610 --> 00:10:00.270
in the blue- and green-suit performers

00:10:00.270 --> 00:10:03.500
standing in for characters
that will be created with CG.

00:10:03.500 --> 00:10:05.978
In 2019's "Dumbo," a green-suit performer

00:10:05.978 --> 00:10:07.569
helped the cast with their sight lines

00:10:07.569 --> 00:10:09.540
when interacting with the baby elephant.

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:13.790
And motion capture allows an
actor to play a CG dog on set,

00:10:13.790 --> 00:10:16.660
so nobody is guessing
where they have to look.

00:10:16.660 --> 00:10:19.390
Looking back at Dynamation's
individual elements,

00:10:19.390 --> 00:10:23.450
each effect can seem time-consuming
and outdated on its own,

00:10:23.450 --> 00:10:27.050
but it shows how every
technological advancement in cinema

00:10:27.050 --> 00:10:29.290
wouldn't exist without
the low-tech experiments

00:10:29.290 --> 00:10:31.207
of the industry's past.

